Open Interpreter **v0.0.61** landed November 11. The changelog is short — just two bullet points — but the signal is massive: **build attestation and provenance verification** for Linux archives.

---

## The Feature That Matters: Your Binary Can Now Prove Its Innocence

**What it is:** Every Linux release artifact now ships with a signed attestation linking it back to the exact tagged build in GitHub Actions. You can verify the checksum manifest against the original build provenance *before* you run it.

**Why it matters:** Supply chain attacks on developer tools are real. Remember the `event-stream` incident? The `xz-utils` backdoor? Attackers compromise the build pipeline, not the source code. Attestation means you — or your package manager, or your CI — can cryptographically verify *this binary came from that commit on that runner at that time*.

**For beginners:** It's a tamper-evident seal. If someone swaps the download on the server, the attestation breaks. You'll know before you pipe it to `bash`.

---

## What's Actually In The Release

| Change | Impact |
|--------|--------|
| Attest Linux archives + checksum manifest in the original tagged build | You can verify provenance before installing |
| Verify original build provenance before publishing existing artifacts | No more "trust me, it's the same binary" |

That's it. Two bullets. But they represent a **baseline shift** for any agent that runs code on your machine.

---

## Why This Matters More Than New Features

Open Interpreter runs *arbitrary code* on your behalf. It spawns shells, installs packages, writes files. If its binary is compromised, the attacker gets everything your user account has.

Most AI coding agents skip this. They ship binaries via GitHub Releases with a "trust us" shrug. Open Interpreter just drew a line: **verifiable builds or bust**.

For teams: this enables `sigstore`/`cosign` verification in your internal mirrors. For solo devs: `cosign verify-blob` against the public transparency log. For everyone: the bar just moved.

---

## How to Verify (If You Care)

```bash
# Download the artifact + attestation
wget https://github.com/openinterpreter/openinterpreter/releases/download/rust-v0.0.61/openinterpreter-linux.tar.gz
wget https://github.com/openinterpreter/openinterpreter/releases/download/rust-v0.0.61/openinterpreter-linux.tar.gz.attestation

# Verify with cosign (requires sigstore setup)
cosign verify-blob \
  --certificate-identity-regexp "https://github.com/openinterpreter/openinterpreter/.github/workflows/.*" \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com" \
  --blob openinterpreter-linux.tar.gz \
  --signature openinterpreter-linux.tar.gz.attestation
```

Or just check the checksum against the manifest in the release notes. The point is: **you can**.

---

## The Bigger Picture

This is v0.0.61. The version number says "early." The security posture says "grown up."

Open Interpreter has been the "run code locally" pioneer since 2023. Adding attestation before 1.0 signals they treat *your machine's integrity* as a blocker, not an afterthought.

Other agents should take notes. If your tool executes code on my hardware, I want a receipt.

---

## How to Update

```bash
# pip (Python wrapper)
pip install --upgrade open-interpreter

# Or download the attested binary directly from GitHub Releases
# https://github.com/openinterpreter/openinterpreter/releases/tag/rust-v0.0.61
```

---

## The Bottom Line

A tiny release. A massive precedent.

**Open Interpreter v0.0.61** doesn't add new models, new skills, or new UI. It adds *trust*. In a category where every agent wants root on your box, that's the feature that actually matters.

